Subject: Re: Multia Documentation

Lennart,

    I checked with one of the Multia doc writers and received the following
    response:
    
	Every site that puchases Multias is supposed to buy
	at least one System Administrator's Kit, which includes
	a System Administrator Information guide and CD-ROM. 
	(If they order a kit, make sure they order the right platform!
	 There are Intel and Alpha versions.)

	The idea is that a system administrator sets up the first desktop
	as a Multia configuration server and preconfigures other
	desktops before users turn them on. The admin assigns a desktop
	key to each desktop he configures. When users turn them on
	the first time, they simply enter their key, and the desktop
	finds its configuration file stored on the server.

	The guide steps you through the process of preconfiguring
	Multias before users turn them on. The guide is also available
	as online help on the Reference Desk. If the customer
	clicks on the RefDesk icon in the Application Manager, he
	can select the Admin Ref. icon.

	If you choose to run Standalone, you can't manage that desktop
	over the network from another Multia. You can still make normal
	network connections, etc.

	QSUSER is a default administrator account. There is no account
	with more privileges.

    Regards,
    Terry
