Subject: Re: Multia Documentation? (SUMMARY)

Thank you all for your kind help.

Even though all my problems have not been solved yet, I think I can now
summarise the basic "how to buy and startup your first Multia". I believe most
of this information is included in the "System Administrators Kit" which I have
not yet received, but below is more-or-less the minimum steps required.

There is no paper version of the documentaion, but the "System Administrators
Kit" contains a postscript version of the Administrators Reference, which is
essential. This kit also contains software for you "mainframes", VMS or Unix.

A: Ordering.
 1: In addition to one Multia, order a System Administrators Kit. I didn't.

 2: Order a CDrom reader. I did.

 3: If you want a dual-head Multia, pick up the latest version of the
 ZLXp-drivers either from Digital or the AlphaNT archive. The drivers delivered
 with the Multia do not work. (I got mine from Digital Service.) 

 4: If you have an Alpha Multia: For all software you ordered, double check
 that you've ordered the Alpha versions. I did. I have none the less received
 the MIPS version of Visual C++, and the Intel version of NuTcracker.

B: Startup.

 1: Interrupt the boot sequence (by hitting any arrow key), select the
 Supplementary menu and display the hardware configuration. Check all disks etc
 are present in the list. (My CDrom reader wasn't. Loose cable inside the
 cabinet.)

 2: While within the boot menues, you might want to change the
 default configuration if you have a language specific keyboard etc. (I have.)

 3: Boot. The magic username/passord is QSUSER/QSUSER. 

 4: From the startup screen, DON'T select "standalone", DONT search for a
 configuration. (The Multia expects to read its configuration from a remote
 "configuration server". If the Multia is your first, it has to became its own
 server.) Close the startup, if you don't want to browse through the tutorial
 first.

 5: If you don't have a printout of the documentation available, open the
 Refdesk, select the Administrators Ref. Lookup "How to became an
 Administrator". Read carefully. Basic steps as follows (only an outline).

  a: From the Multia menu, select "Configure this desktop". You get a
  popup-menue if you click on the picture of the Multia system unit to the
  right. Use this menue to configure TCPIP, DECnet or whatever will be your
  preferred network protocol. Select a Computer Name. Now configure Services
  (the Lorry icon). Select "Configuration server". You must now enter
  appropiate startup parameters BEFORE starting the server. The Help lists the
  alternatives, but makes no recommendations. You MUST select a network
  protocol (-p), using the rather cryptic names listed in the help, and an
  appropiate endpoint (-e). You do probably want a password to protect your
  server (-i). You do not need the select a path. Start the server. Close all
  configuration windows.

  b: You must now open the Config application and repeat the configuration.
  Config should find one configuration server (itself). Select it. Use Add to
  create a workgroup. Select it. Use Add to create a desktop, your own.
  Select it, open Status, open Config. Now repeat the configuration in step 5a.

  c: Restart. When you login again, and the startup screen activates, use it to
  search for the configuration server (itself). It will configure and restart
  again.

 6: Your Multia has now become its own configuration server. Proceed at will...
 (If you have bought the Xserver option, it must be installed separately from
 the System Administrators Kit.)

/Cheers, Lennart

