-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
November 13, 1995 

Unix users get fast path to Windows
***********************************

By Ken Phillips 

Beta Sight
----------

Effectively solving problems that have exasperated administrators of
multiplatform enterprises for years, NCD Systems Corp.'s WinCenter Pro 1.0
gives users of X-based client machines full-blast access to any Windows
application across the WAN. No longer must a user's platform determine the
applications he or she can run. 

PC Week Labs examined a beta version of WinCenter Pro, slated to ship Nov. 30
for $395 per seat (15-user minimum), and deemed it reliable and
maintenance-free. What's more, it allows nearly all Windows applications to run
at top speed, though paint and photo-manipulation packages may run slightly
more slowly. 

It's scalable--to add more users, just add more server memory and CPUs.
Applications and data can be installed at one computer, avoiding replication,
and an entire enterprise can standardize on a single E-mail system or word
processor, regardless of platform. 

The benefits go on: Cut-and-paste between Unix and Windows is a trivial matter.

Security is managed through Windows NT, and the administration of both PC and
Unix desktops is centralized. Performance-hungry users can be satisfied with
high-throughput terminals, while the bulk of users may use lower-cost
terminals. 

Best of all, WinCenter Pro does not clutter the Unix environment one iota. It
resides on a multiuser Windows NT system--Citrix Systems Inc.'s WinFrame 1.50,
which is packaged with WinCenter Pro. This approach serves Unix users via the
open protocols TCP/IP and the X Window System. 

How does it do that? ==================== 

Injecting broad new functionality into ordinary TCP/IP networks with no
disruption or complaints, WinCenter Pro uses the tried-and-true (and
non-proprietary) X protocol over TCP/IP as a means of transmitting user input
and graphical display output between the components of client/server
applications. 

As detailed in the accompanying story at right, it translates X-format requests
from X clients into the protocol understood by WinFrame, and translates the
Windows display output back into X. It supports up to 16 million colors, plus
monochrome displays. 

Network Computing Devices Inc., a leading X-terminal and PC-based X-server
software manufacturer and owner of NCD Systems Corp., jointly developed this
technology with Insignia Solutions Inc., the primary vendor of PC/Windows
emulation software for Unix. Insignia has announced its own packaging of this
technology, called NTrigue, but no release date was available at press time. 

Up to now, the attempts to place Windows applications on Unix desktops have
been workable but limited. For instance, Wabi 2.1 provides a Windows
replacement to run under Solaris or SCO Unix, translating Windows system calls
into Unix equivalents and allowing terminal-based users to have their own
Windows sessions. 

Unfortunately, there's no guarantee that all Windows apps will run----each must
be certified individually. 

The number of shrink-wrapped applications that run under Wabi is still limited
to the two dozen most popular titles or suites. 

In addition, its "Windows" GUI also presents an unnatural look and feel. 

There's also Insignia Solutions' just-announced SoftWindows 2.0 for Unix, which
runs all DOS and Windows applications on a Unix workstation; instead of
translating system calls, it utilizes true-blue Windows and DOS, emulating the
PC hardware behind the scenes. 

However, this extra processing burden cuts the performance of applications to a
speed resembling a low-end 486, and limits users to those seated at Unix
workstations only--not attached terminals. 

Currently, neither SoftWindows nor Wabi can run Windows 95 or Windows NT
applications. 

To provide the multiuser functionality, Citrix Systems Inc.'s NT-based WinFrame
1.5 access server is included with WinCenter Pro. 

WinFrame retrofits old, low-performance (even diskless) PCs and new low-cost
Windows terminals to perform as full-fledged Windows seats; it also provides
remote access to Windows via dial-up lines, while TCP/IP is the transport for
local network connections. (A review of WinFrame appears in Netweek's Aug. 28
issue, Page N1.) 

However, the optional PC client retrofit and remote access software is not
included in the WinCenter Pro version. 

The WinFrame/WinCenter Pro team gives users access to ancient Windows
applications as well as new 32-bit Windows 95 and Windows NT iterations.
Because it runs on its own NT system, there's no performance drain or
configuration to enact at any Unix server, leaving them devoted entirely to
mission-critical applications. 

Install and let 'er rip ======================= 

Not only was no maintenance required, but we also had no trouble installing the
WinCenter Pro 1.0 beta on our AT&T Global Information Solutions Globalyst 630
PC, a 120MHz Pentium system equipped with 16M bytes of RAM plus Citrix's
WinFrame 1.50. WinCenter Pro required no configuration other than our accepting
some defaults in WinFrame's WinStation Configuration tool. 

The Globalyst server was connected via 10Base2 cabling to a TCP/IP network
consisting of a Sun Microsystems Computer Corp. SPARCstation LX workstation and
a high-performance NCD HMXpro X terminal that booted from flash memory. Merging
a multiuser Windows NT box into a TCP/IP network is a simple matter, because NT
includes the TCP/IP protocol stack. 

We were able to connect to WinCenter Pro from either Unix station without
experiencing a bit of hesitation. 

At the SPARCstation LX, we merely typed a remote shell command to initiate a
Windows session. The HMXpro automatically presented the NT host in its Login
Chooser window, due to WinCenter Pro's X Display Manager protocol support.
Users can also start a session via Telnet. 

Our HMXpro X terminal could as easily have been called a Windows terminal, for
the I/O had a thoroughly Windows look and feel. 

Indeed, so-called Internet terminals, Wyse Technology Inc. WinTerms, Macs, and
any X-capable device can be used with WinCenter Pro. 

We ran Windows-based word processors, spreadsheets, and planners as if seated
at a full-fledged PC. At the Sun workstation, it was a non-event to cut and
paste information between Solaris and Windows applications; standard procedures
are used. 

We also browsed the Web via our U.S. Robotics Inc. Courier V.34 fax modem
attached to the WinFrame host. 

Printing through the WinFrame box's local printer required no extra setup, nor
did accessing the PC's other peripherals, such as its CD ROM drive. The only
problems we encountered were minor WinFrame anomalies. 

NCD Systems states that WinCenter Pro requires 4M to 6M bytes of RAM per user
at the server (for average applications) and no more than 10 to 12 users per
CPU for full performance. 

A Pentium server stuffed with four CPUs and 256M bytes of RAM can therefore
support 40 full-time users or more than double that number of one-third-time
users. User desktops require no additional memory. 

Untested extras =============== 

Some features in the WinCenter Pro beta were not ready for our examination. 

For Unix users who need a local floppy drive, NCD Systems has incorporated
bidirectional parallel ports on its newer terminals for connecting an external
floppy drive, along with a utility in WinCenter Pro for accessing the floppy
from Windows. 

An integrated Network File System client will reportedly allow X stations to
mount remote Unix drives and printers while logged in to WinFrame, while a
Trivial File Transport Protocol server aemon should let users boot terminals
without flash memory cards or Unix host access. 

Via remote commands (rsh, rexec, and rcp daemons), a user can reportedly
execute a Windows program without even a formal WinFrame log-in. 

For example, if a Unix user receives a Microsoft Excel spreadsheet as an
attachment to E-mail, he or she can auto-launch Excel by clicking on the
attachment, in much the same way as an existing Windows user launches his or
her applications from within a Windows session. 

And to provide the so-called cheap seats, NCD Systems also plans to
concurrently introduce the Explora, a $995 (includes display) terminal designed
for accessing multiple servers simultaneously. 

This small unit offers roughly 25 percent to 30 percent the performance of the
beefier, $3,995 HMXpro. 

In addition to exploiting WinCenter Pro, the Explora affords Telnet, TN3270,
and VT320 terminal emulation for simultaneous access to legacy, Unix, and
Windows applications. 

NCD Systems, of Mountain View, Calif., can be reached at (415) 694-0650. 

Clicking right along under Unix =============================== 

By Ken Phillips 

After withstanding the often tedious waits of predecessor emulation products
for Unix, we found the speed with which Windows applications responded via
WinCenter Pro to be absolutely refreshing. 

Rather than emulating PC hardware in the background, WinCenter Pro translates X
Window System-format requests originating from the X client user into the
protocol understood by Citrix Systems Inc.'s WinFrame. 

It then works at the video-driver level to send back the Windows display output
translated into X. Because X is an efficient protocol, and because the WinFrame
server need not prepare displays for its own local console, the resultant speed
over the network is close to that at the native server console. 

To see how noticeable the speed hit was, we installed and ran several old and
new Windows software packages, including Microsoft Corp.'s Word 6.0, MS Excel
5.0, MS PowerPoint 4.0, Aldus PageMaker 5.0, NetManage Inc.'s ECCO Professional
2.0, Symantec Corp.'s TimeLine 1.0, and Frontier Technologies Corp.'s
SuperHighway Access CyberSearch 95.09. Happily, all performed at full speed
without forcing noticeable delays. 

Certain types of applications may run more slowly: Not all video commands, such
as a paint program's flood fills, for example, are translatable under X, and
this forces much extra communication between the NT server and the terminal.
For instance, we ran PaintBrush and observed slight lags in response time for
some functions. 

To take an even closer look, we ran Ziff-Davis Benchmark Operations' WinBench
95 1.0 and it reported, overall, only half the pixel throughput of the WinFrame
console itself. This is due to WinBench's constant changes of state between
tests, which cannot be handled as simply under X as by local video hardware. 

However, normal applications avoid frequently changing state. When we ran Texas
Instruments Inc.'s WinTach benchmark, which performs repetitive functions that
more closely mimic real applications, the WinCenter Pro connection at the
HMXpro slightly outperformed the WinFrame console. (The HMXpro is a 430K+
Xstone high-performance terminal.) 

Disk-access (such as for program loading) and number-crunching (such as
spreadsheet calculations) processes ran as fast or faster than normal, due to
the fact that the CPU did not have to manage a local display. LAN traffic may
also decrease overall, because programs run at the server rather than being
downloaded to the user's machine. 

