Dear Frustrated in Los Angeles,

I use several  things for remote administration.

1. SLnet -- allows me to remotely log into the NT system from non-NT hosts 
that support telnet.
2. rcmdsvc & rcmd - the remote command utility from the resource kit
3. NETSVC - load, unload, and control system services remotely
4. REGEDT32 - to set all those settings that I can't get to otherwise

The NET commands do an awful lot, but not everything.  For a lot of the 
settings that you're talking about, you'll need to use the registry editor. 
 [WARNING: use of the Registry editor will result in  multiple paranoid 
warnings from Microsoft about using the Registry editor.]

Supposedly there's a couple products from TI and Centrix (Centrex?) that 
allows for remote execution of NT applications, but I've heard mixed reviews 
on them, nor do  I  know the status of the Alpha recompiles.  The TI product 
uses X, so you can run remote apps from any X based system (hey, finally a 
good use for a PowerMac -- a remote X terminal  for NT systems admin! ;^)

Finally, SHUTDOWN from the resource kit allows me to reboot a machine with 
its new settings.

Good luck,
Aaron
