From rich@promo.nucmed.buffalo.edu Thu Feb 15 12:43:43 1996 CST
Message-ID: <1996q1-0343.alphant@archive.invalid>
X-Archive-Original-Message-ID: <3123A6BC@smtp.nucmed.buffalo.edu>
Date: Thu, 15 Feb 96 13:37:00 PST
From: rich <rich@promo.nucmed.buffalo.edu>
Subject: Modem Problem Solved

POST

I just wanted to let folks know that appreciate having this service 
available and to also thank
folks who responded to me.

I also just wanted to let people know that I solved my problem and what I 
found.

Situation:

I wanted to install 2 28.8 modems in my Alpha 1000 for RAS.

1)  You must run the ECU disk to configure the ISA slot.  The ECU disk will 
tell you which
interrupt to use.  First modem was set to COM 3 and IRQ 5.

2)  Problem came with configuring the second modem.  It took me awhile to 
figure out that the
ECU disk wanted to assign the slot to IRQ 2 / Trigger 9.  This meant that I 
had to add COM Port
4 to my ports in the control panel, BUT HAD TO ASSIGN IT IRQ 9.

Once I did the above then I was able to get RAS working on my Alpha.

Just a somewhat related issue:

The new version of Filemakerpro 3 now is fully relational and supports 
TCP/IP.  However, as
many of you know RAS does not support appletalk.  Thus, a mac can dial in to 
the NT
Server but cannot mount disk services.

However, if you have a filemakerpro 3 file open then the computer that 
opened this file is acting
as a host.  Thus, macs can open up the file by specifying a a specific 
tcp/ip address.

You should note that this bypasses the security on your nt server.  That is 
anyone can get to this
file if they know the ip address.  That is why you must use Filemakerpro's 
security.

Again thanks to all.

Sincerely,

Rich Kayton
SUNY at Buffalo
Dept of Nuclear Medicine

