From: rich <rich@promo.nucmed.buffalo.edu>
Subject: Problem Solved

POST

I just want to AGAIN thank everyone for having this site available and to 
especially thank those
of you who tried to help me install a new hard drive in my DEC Alpha 1000.

The solution involved 2 things:

1) I had to send my drive back to Seagate for Repair or Checkup. 
 Unfortunately, I do not really know if my original drive was broken.  All I 
do know is that I did not receive my original drive back.

2)  I had to know how to correctly set the jumpers on my Seagate drive.

This may apply to others so here is what I found out.

There are several jumpers that must be set on the Seagate drive.  Just a 
reminder that on the Alpha you enclose the drive in a plastic case.

You must take off all jumpers that apply termination since that is handled 
by the plastic case.
The really important jumper is the "delay motor start" jumper.  This is the 
jumper that must be used.  There is another jumper that named "enable motor 
start" that should NOT be used.
Contrary to what you expect the delay motor start is used so as not to 
overpower the power supply.  It delays the motor startup 12 seconds  X SCSI 
ID address.  The enable motor start
actually waits for a command from the computer that is never sent on the 
Alpha.

Anyway, I was able to create a partition and format the drive.  Now I am 
testing it.

Thanks again,

Rich Kayton
SUNY at Buffalo
Dept. of Nuclear Medicine

