From: Burt Knight <BurtKnight@ichange.com>
Subject: NYB and Viruses in General

>What are the symptoms of the virus?  The only scan program I have is 
almost 
>a year old.  Is there a way I can check for it "manually"?
>(ERIC S. MUCKLOW - RICK)

I'd recommend getting either McAfee or Norton.  Both are available in 
'Demo'
versions.  The URL for Norton is 
http://www.symantec.com/virus/virus.html.  
McAfee's URL is http://www.mcafee.com.  Both are good performers and I
use both in my scans.  Another good AVtool is F-Prot, 
http://www.datafellows.fi/f-prot.htm.

I spent a bit of time on the 'virus' team at Ziffnet and saw a number of
different viruses.  We scanned all files with four to five scanners 
(McAfee,
Norton, Thunderbyte, F-Prot and AIDSTest <a Russian AV tool I played 
with
for a while>).  Because we were commercially distributing files, we 
wanted
to insure that we had taken all precautions.  On a server, I'd recommend
scanning with two up-to-date scanners - companies are continually 
issuing
updates for registered users.

NYB is among the milder strains I've seen and I'm really perplexed by 
the
number of times I have seen it in the past year on New Installs.  From 
Norton's description:
	'NYB, alias B1
	Infects Floppy and Master Boot Records
	Size: 512 bytes
	Likelihood: Common
	Memory resident, Size & Full Stealth

	NYB is a fairly generic Floppy Boot Sector and MBR infector. It
	goes resident but does not destroy anything intentionally.'

I can tell you the story of a Virus Horror that could have been worse 
but was bad enough.  Being the 'discoverer' of a New strain of virus 
and being allowed to 'name' the sucker is an 'honour' I didn't want 
and would gladly forego again.  Fortunately we were able to stop 
the virus before it went too far.

Enough of that...  Get a current AV tool and scan regularly.  It won't 
stop them all (my horror was undetectable because no one had ever
seen it before) but the precautions are worth it.  Also never do 
anything with your file structures without scanning the drives first -
the one side 'benefit' of NYB Norton didn't find was that it will delete
files being 'compressed' on NTFS partitions (I think that's what 
happened to me).

BWK
-->Unix is User-friendly, just VERY picky about its friends<--

